4.85.m:
Born on earth as a Yakṣa, one who steals articles used in play
becomes surely a blue jay for a hundred years.
Then he becomes a black-skinned Śūdra in the land of Bhārata.
Then he becomes a Brahmin, yet having extra limbs in that birth;
born once again as a twice-born, he is purified by feeding Brahmins.
One who steals cooked food surely attains the womb of a beast
whose scrotum is smeared with fragrance, known as musk.
Having been a deer for seven births, he then becomes a musk deer for one birth;
then he becomes a Śūdra afflicted with weeping leprosy in that birth.
Then, endowed with the remnants of the disease, he is a frail Brahmin;
by giving six palas of gold, he is freed—of this there is no doubt.
A stealer of grain is miserable and wretched for seven births;
having reached the Pit of Feces for a hundred years, he is released in fear.
A stealer of gold becomes a leper and a fallen man.
One who accepts an improper gift of gold goes to the Pit of Excrement;
then, having fed on feces day and night for a hundred years,
he then becomes a Śūdra tiger afflicted with a blood disorder.
Having endured the sin of that birth, he becomes a Brahmin again;
joined with disease and anger, he is freed by giving gold.
One who approaches forbidden women goes to the previously mentioned Raurava
and to the most dreadful Kumbhīpāka for countless years.
Then he becomes a worm in the wombs of unchaste women
for a thousand years, and a feces-worm for a hundred thousand years.
From that he attains an animal womb, and from that [the form of] tiny vermin;
then he is born into a Mleccha caste, and then as the lowest of Śūdras.
Then he becomes a Brahmin who is afflicted with disease and is a eunuch;
becoming a Brahmin once more, by wandering to holy places,
he gradually becomes pure, though bereft of lineage due to his sin;
having fed a hundred thousand Brahmins, the purified man attains a son.

A wrathful man becomes a donkey for seven births.
A quarrelsome man becomes a crow for seven births.
One who accepts a Śāligrāma [improperly] surely goes to Kālasūtra;
then for a hundred years he becomes a wagtail.
A thief of iron is without offspring; a thief of ink becomes a cuckoo;
a thief of collyrium becomes a parrot; and a thief of sweets becomes a worm.
A hater of Brahmins and a hater of the guru becomes a head louse.
Having enjoyed a lustful, unchaste woman, O dear one, he goes to Raurava;
